-- ToStrata: a unit's typed tree, as inference leaves it, written as terms of
-- Strata's core (`Strata.Core`), with everything at run time and every
-- binder of grade ω (`docs/2LTT.md`, L1). `StrataCheck` has Strata's
-- checker (`Strata.Check`) check what it writes.
--
-- It runs only under the `strata` option: what Cut is lowered from is still
-- `Elab`'s core. A construct the core has nothing for is not an error: it is
-- written as a goal, `unmapped:<kind>`, which the dump counts by kind
-- (`MEADOWBOOT_STRATA`), and a declaration with one in it is not checked.
--
-- What maps, and how:
--
-- - a definition is a constant, by its binder, of the type its scheme says:
--   each quantifier an implicit Pi over `Type` (or `Effect`, for an effect
--   row's), each trait it asks for an auto Pi over the trait's dictionary
--   type, each variable over number types an auto Pi over a dictionary of
--   `#Num` (or `#Frac`), and then its type, each arrow a Pi and each latent
--   effect a `Comp` with a `Row`;
-- - its body takes those as lambdas, the types first, then the dictionaries;
--   a mention of a generic name is applied to the types it is used at --
--   effects and rows too, read off the type it is used at -- and to the
--   dictionaries inference found for it, so the core is explicit;
-- - a local is a de Bruijn index, and a top-level name, a constructor or a
--   type an `Opaque` by its binder: so top-level recursion, mutual or not,
--   needs nothing more. A local function that calls itself is lifted to a
--   constant of its own, which takes everything in scope where it was
--   defined as parameters first;
-- - a `match` is a tree of one-level `case`s (`compile`): a constructor
--   splits on every constructor of its type, a literal is a test of
--   equality, a view is its function applied, and a guard falls through to
--   the arms after it;
-- - a tuple is a data type of one constructor for each arity, `TupleN`, and
--   a structural record a data type of one constructor for each set of
--   labels, `#rec{a,b}`, its fields in the labels' order;
-- - a named record is a data type of one constructor, read by a `case`;
-- - a trait's dictionary is a data type of one constructor, a method generic
--   in more than its trait a field generic in the rest; an `impl` is a
--   definition of a dictionary, built of what its own `where` asks for, the
--   dictionaries of the traits its trait requires, and each method -- its
--   own, or the trait's default;
-- - a `handle` is a `Handle`, each clause a function of the operation's
--   argument and of its resumption, as Idyll's are;
-- - `runSt`'s argument is generic in the state, which inference made a rigid
--   type of its own (`s#N`): the translation binds it there.
--
-- Meadow's own types and effects, which no package declares (`Array`, `St`,
-- `Mut` and the rest), are constants of uniques of their own, above every
-- binder (`synthetic`), as are the tuples, the records, and what the
-- translation needs that Meadow has no binder for: a list made an array
-- (`#array`), and the number types' dictionaries.

